      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the given ellipse, we need to determine the values of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\).\n\nThe standard form of an ellipse is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{b^2} + \\frac{y^2}{a^2} = 1\n\\]\ndepending on whether the major axis is along the x-axis or y-axis.\n\nFrom the given problem, we can see that the ellipse is centered at \\((1, 1)\\) and is oriented such that the semi-major axis is along the x-axis and the semi-minor axis is along the y-axis. Thus, the standard form of the equation of the ellipse is:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-1)^2}{6^2} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{2^2} = 1\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-1)^2}{2^2} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{6^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 2\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is calculated using the relationship:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate \\(c\\):\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{36 - 4} = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\cdot 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\(\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\\)",
